WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:02.980
A TCP service is exposed software.

2
00:00:03.080 --> 00:00:09.720
It accepts data from another process, another host, or another network segment and turns that data into program behavior.

3
00:00:09.820 --> 00:00:21.500
In C, that boundary matters because network input often reaches parsing code, memory buffers, state machines, resource allocation, logs, child work, and cleanup paths.

4
00:00:21.600 --> 00:00:26.480
Even a service with only a few commands can carry many security-relevant assumptions.

5
00:00:26.760 --> 00:00:34.200
The first defensive habit is to treat every connection as untrusted until the service has validated what it knows.

6
00:00:34.300 --> 00:00:43.580
The remote peer may be an expected application, an outdated client, a misconfigured integration, a test tool, or simply a connection that sends incomplete data.

7
00:00:43.680 --> 00:00:51.040
The service should not assume that bytes arrive in useful chunks, that fields are well formed, or that a client follows the happy path.

8
00:00:51.320 --> 00:00:54.280
Small services still need explicit design.

9
00:00:54.380 --> 00:01:10.380
A simple administrative listener, local helper, queue bridge, metrics endpoint, or embedded control service may be treated as internal, but it still has a listening scope, a protocol contract, resource limits, error behavior, and logging choices.

10
00:01:10.480 --> 00:01:14.000
Internal exposure is not the same as no exposure.

11
00:01:14.280 --> 00:01:17.800
Secure TCP service design connects several disciplines.

12
00:01:17.900 --> 00:01:21.580
Parser boundaries prevent ambiguous input handling.

13
00:01:21.680 --> 00:01:25.200
Memory ownership prevents unsafe buffer use.

14
00:01:25.300 --> 00:01:30.260
Resource limits keep descriptors, memory, and per-client work predictable.

15
00:01:30.360 --> 00:01:35.400
Authentication and encryption protect trust decisions where they are needed.

16
00:01:35.500 --> 00:01:42.380
Logging and diagnostics make behavior observable without exposing secrets or recording excessive client-controlled content.

17
00:01:42.660 --> 00:01:46.980
This course focuses on the engineering habits that make a C service reviewable.

18
00:01:47.080 --> 00:01:57.880
Define what listens, where it listens, who is expected to connect, how messages are framed, how data is validated, how connections are cleaned up, and how failures are handled.

19
00:01:57.980 --> 00:02:03.660
A service becomes safer when those answers are deliberate rather than accidental.
